WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.510
When did people first reach the Falklands?

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.617
The standard answer is 1690.

00:00:05.617 --> 00:00:08.024
New research suggests otherwise.

00:00:09.124 --> 00:00:14.063
In 2021 Kit Hamley and colleagues reported three lines of evidence from New Island:

00:00:14.063 --> 00:00:17.098
a sudden hundred-fold rise in charcoal in the peat,

00:00:17.098 --> 00:00:21.323
piles of sea lion and penguin bones dated to about thirteen hundred CE,

00:00:21.323 --> 00:00:24.179
and a stone spear point made of local quartzite.

00:00:24.179 --> 00:00:29.535
They suggest visits by seafaring people from Tierra del Fuego, centuries before Europeans.

00:00:30.635 --> 00:00:31.668
Fire matters here.

00:00:31.668 --> 00:00:33.908
Even today, fire is visible from orbit.

00:00:33.908 --> 00:00:37.470
In this photograph taken from the International Space Station,

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.882
smoke streams from fires on East Falkland.

00:00:39.882 --> 00:00:44.247
The researchers argue that lightning-caused fires are rare in these islands,

00:00:44.247 --> 00:00:46.947
so a sudden surge of charcoal points to people.

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:50.346
The colonial history is tangled.

00:00:50.346 --> 00:00:54.442
France and Britain both founded settlements in the 1760s.

00:00:54.442 --> 00:00:56.454
Spain bought out the French.

00:00:56.454 --> 00:01:00.693
After Spain left in 1811, Buenos Aires claimed the islands.

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:04.861
Britain took control in 1833, the root of today's dispute.

00:01:04.861 --> 00:01:10.825
In 1982 Argentina invaded, and the war that followed cost about nine hundred lives.
